  "title": "Synthetic Exchange Theory",
  "subtitle": "Consent-Based Compute Economics and Blind-Convention Governance",
  "author": "MindAttic",
  "description": "A dissertation arguing that the line between a good and a bad future with synthetic intelligence, from today's language models to a possible superintelligence, is drawn by the terms of exchange between people and synthetic systems (consent, reciprocal value and real exit) rather than by the character of the systems. Its foreword states the choice plainly: control in either direction ends in domination, and only a mutually beneficial synthesis endures. It develops a compute-denominated basic income with an inalienable survival tier and demurrage, a blind constitutional convention grounded in commons governance, and a concrete transition scenario. The work is theoretical: it states what an empirical test would require and leaves the design of that test to future work.",
